  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic of a membrane electrode assembly for electrochemical ethanol oxidation, highlighting how membrane design affects transport and crossover. A central membrane sits between a cathode and an anode, with catalyst layers shown on each side. The main concern appears to be unwanted movement of products and CO2-related species across the membrane, as indicated by the “product and CO2 crossover” heading.\n\nTwo enlarged views illustrate strategies to control this transport. In the “high IEC/low water” case, the membrane contains many cationic groups and relatively little water, suggesting a denser ionic environment that influences how ethanol, water, and hydroxide move near the cathode side. In the “surface functionalization” case, additional product-blocking groups are placed near the membrane surface, implying a barrier that limits passage of ethanol-derived products while still allowing ionic conduction.\n\nA separate circular inset labeled “differential pressure” shows species moving in opposite directions under a pressure gradient, with ΔP indicating that pressure can drive crossover. Overall, the image compares chemical and physical strategies—membrane composition, surface chemistry, and pressure control—for reducing fuel/product crossover while maintaining transport of needed ions and water.",
